messages:  [['Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B or C. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nIf people work in student jobs on campus, then they need to earn money to help pay for their college tuition. If people order takeout frequently in college, then they work in student jobs on campus. People either order takeout frequently in college or enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes. If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they are not picky eaters. If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they spend a lot of their time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls. If Mary works in student jobs on campus and needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition, then Mary is neither picky nor needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, is the following statement true, false, or uncertain? Mary either is not a picky eater or, if she is, then she spends a lot of her time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls.\n# Options:\nA) True\nB) False\nC) Uncertain. I think the correct option is: A\n', '<think>.\nTo solve this logical reasoning problem, let\'s break down the given information and analyze the statement step by step.\n\n1. **Context Analysis:**.\n   - If people work in student jobs on campus, then they need to earn money to help pay for their college tuition.\n   - If people order takeout frequently in college, then they work in student jobs on campus.\n   - People either order takeout frequently in college or enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes.\n   - If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they are not picky eaters.\n   - If people enjoy the dining hall meals and recipes, then they spend a lot of their time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls.\n   - Mary works in student jobs on campus and needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.\n   - If Mary works in student jobs on campus and needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition, then Mary is neither picky nor needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.\n\n2. **Statement to Evaluate:**.\n   - Mary either is not a picky eater or, if she is, then she spends a lot of her time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls.\n\n3. **Logical Deductions:**.\n   - From the context, we know that if Mary works in student jobs on campus and needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition, then she is neither picky nor needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition.\n   - This implies that Mary is not picky (since she is neither picky nor needs to earn money to help pay for her college tuition).\n\n4. **Analyzing the Statement:**.\n   - The statement says: "Mary either is not a picky eater or, if she is, then she spends a lot of her time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls.".\n   - Since we deduced that Mary is not picky, the first part of the statement ("Mary either is not a picky eater") is true.\n   - The second part of the statement ("if she is, then she spends a lot of her time eating and catching up with friends in the campus dining halls") is not relevant because we already know Mary is not picky.\n\n5. **Conclusion:**.\n   - The statement is true because the first part of the statement is true, and the second part is not applicable since Mary is not picky. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
